## Changes applied (2026-09-03) — struct shrink
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Goal was item 2's deferred work: cut per-lane state so more work-groups stay
resident per SM (throughput plateaued at 512 resident threads/SM of 1,536).

Measured instance size (host C build of the kernel, arm64, 1-byte bool):
3984 -> 1504 bytes, a 62% reduction. NVIDIA's 4-byte bool makes the old
number 5552, so the relative win there is larger.

    member            before   after
    seed                  80      80   (unchanged, see "not applied" below)
    cache rngCache      3080    1032
    hashedSeed             8       8
    lrandom rng           48      48
    locked[]             494      64   (was bool[494], now ulong[8])
    params               260     260
    total               3984    1504

### 1. locked[] is a bitset (lib/instance.cl)
`bool locked[ITEMS_END]` became `ulong locked[LOCKED_WORDS]` where
LOCKED_WORDS = (ITEMS_END + 63) / 64 = 8, with three inline accessors
declared right after the struct: i_locked / i_lock / i_unlock. 190 call
sites were converted mechanically (173 writes, 17 reads):

    lib/instance.cl            140   (mostly init_locks / init_unlocks)
    lib/functions.cl            30
    filters/analyzer.cl         16
    filters/purchaseless.cl      3
    filters/max_cash_ante_1.cl   1

i_new now zeroes the 8 words and calls i_lock(inst, RETRY), matching the old
`{.locked = {true}}` semantics exactly (only locked[0] set). No
`inst->locked[...]` site remains outside the accessors themselves.
docs/DOCUMENTATION.md describes a lock/unlock/isLocked API without naming the
struct field, so it needed no change.

### 2. Compact RNG cache node (lib/cache.cl, lib/instance.cl)
`rnginfo` was `{ntype nodeTypes[4]; int nodeValues[4]; int depth; double
rngState;}` (48 bytes) and is now `{ulong key; double rngState;}` (16 bytes),
so the 64-node cache drops from 3080 to 1032 bytes.

The key packs the (type, value) pairs and the depth into one word: 16 bits
per slot, slot k at bits 16k..16k+15, holding ((nodeType & 3) << 14) |
(nodeValue & 0x3FFF). Slots at or past the node's depth are 0xFFFF, which no
real slot can produce because every nodeValue in use is far below 0x3FFF
(rtype < 40, rsrc < 24, antes run 1..8, resample counters are small) — so the
key is injective over the values this kernel actually generates and encodes
the depth implicitly. get_node_child builds the key once and the lookup loop
is now a single 64-bit compare per cached node instead of a nested loop over
up to 4 type/value pairs. init_node stores the key. The string fed to
pseudohash is built exactly as before; only the lookup key changed.

Overflow guard: init_node previously wrote past nodes[] once nextFreeNode
reached CACHE_SIZE, silently. It now prints one line naming CACHE_SIZE (gated
by a new `reportedOverflow` bool in the cache struct, initialized false in
i_new, so it is at most one message per work-item) and reuses the last slot
instead of writing out of bounds. Results for an overflowing seed are still
wrong, but memory is no longer corrupted. Confirmed under AddressSanitizer:
the old code reports a stack-buffer-overflow at CACHE_SIZE=8, the new code
does not. CACHE_SIZE also became a normal `#ifndef`-guarded define (default
64) rather than an #ifdef in the struct body; filters that set it (analyzer,
perkeo_analyzer: 1000) are unaffected.

### 3. Seed digits as bytes — NOT APPLIED (reverted)
Changing `seed.data` from ulong8 to uchar8 changes search output and cannot
be done as a pure storage change. s_skip relies on digits being able to hold
-1:

    sum += s->data[i * (i >= 0)] * (i >= 0) + -1 * (i < 0);
    data[j] = sum % NUM_CHARS;

When the skip distance has more base-35 digits than the current seed length,
i goes negative, sum becomes -1, and C's truncating % stores -1 into the
digit array. s_char_at then indexes SEEDCHARS[-1] and s_to_string emits the
byte before the string literal — in practice a NUL, which truncates the
printed seed. With ulong8 that -1 is stored as 0xFFFF...FF; with uchar8 it
becomes 255, a different out-of-range index, so the two builds print
different seed strings and, because the string is what pseudohash consumes,
different scores.

Verified by differential test: with an empty or 1-2 character starting seed
and a stride of one launch's worth of work-items (43,008), the base and
uchar8 builds diverge from the second seed onward. The default starting seed
IS the empty seed, so this is on the normal path, not a corner case. Start
seeds of 3+ characters were identical for every stride tested.

This is a pre-existing bug in s_skip, not something the byte change
introduced — the old build is already printing truncated seed strings for
short start seeds — but fixing it changes output, so it is out of scope for
a size reduction that must stay byte-identical. Left as a separate item; see
"Deferred" below. Reverting it costs 72 of the targeted bytes (the struct is
1504 rather than the ~1.2 KB target).

