## Changes applied (2026-09-03) — s_skip correctness fix and filter deletions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### s_skip rewritten via the seed rank (lib/seed.cl)
Seeds are ordered in bijective base 35: rank 0 is the empty seed, 1..35 the
one-character seeds, 36..1260 the two-character seeds, up to rank
2,318,107,019,760 = "ZZZZZZZZ". s_tell now returns that rank (the old s_tell
was dead code and wrong: it multiplied by a `mult` that started at 0),
s_from_rank is its inverse, and s_skip(s, n) is simply
s_from_rank(s_tell(s) + n).

Why: the old digit-wise s_skip, when it had to grow the seed by a digit whose
computed value was -1, stored the -1 as a digit. s_to_string then indexed
SEEDCHARS[-1], producing a corrupt string that pseudohash consumed, so every
seed that work-item visited for the rest of the run was wrong. Measured on
the host with the old code: with the default empty starting seed, 7 of 256
work-items under the old geometry and 3,609 of 43,008 under the new one
started on a corrupt seed (roughly 1 in 12). Any 8-character -s value was
unaffected, which is why the earlier golden runs were clean.

Host-side differential test against the old s_skip:
- 8-character start seeds, six strides from 1 to 33,554,432, 2,000 steps
  each: identical on all 48,007 in-range steps. The 11,993 mismatches were
  all past "ZZZZZZZZ", where the old code grew a 9th digit; the new code
  stays at length 8 (the kernel's -n bound never reaches there anyway).
- Short start seeds ("", "1", "Z", "AB", "ZZZ", "1234"): wherever the old
  code produced valid digits the two agree (107,960 cases, 0 disagreements);
  the 40 cases where the old code was corrupt are now valid seeds.
- 200,000 unit steps from the empty seed match s_from_rank(k) exactly and
  contain no invalid digit; s_tell(s_from_rank(r)) == r for 430k sampled
