## Measurement (2026-09-03) — struct shrink had no throughput effect
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTX 5080, -s 11111111, groups of 32, 1344 groups:
    erratic_flush_five  500M seeds  -c 11   before shrink 3.55 s, after 3.5 s
    perkeo_observatory  100M seeds  -c 1    ~0.5 s both
    erratic_flush_five with #define CACHE_SIZE 4 (per-lane struct ~600 B): 3.5 s
Output identical to HEAD in every case.

Conclusion: per-lane memory is not what caps occupancy. Halving the struct,
then cutting it by a further 60%, moved nothing. The remaining ceiling is
compute: either the register footprint of the inlined fp64 kernel limits
resident warps (consistent with the driver rejecting 64-wide groups), or the
1/64-rate fp64 units are saturated by the ~75 divides and ~150 multiplies
per seed. Both point the same way: the next gain must come from doing less
work per seed, not from fitting more lanes. Deferred items 3 and 4
(CACHE_SIZE, bitset) are done and closed; item 7 (jump-ahead) is next.

