## Measurement (2026-09-03) — where the time actually goes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTX 5080, -s 11111111 -n 500000000 -c 11, 1344 groups of 32:

    erratic_flush_five (full)     3.02 s   100%
    bench_hash_fp64               1.85 s    61%   52 node advances via get_node_child, no RNG
    bench_rng_int                 0.88 s    29%   52 randomseed + draw, no hashing
    remainder (i_new, s_skip,    ~0.30 s    10%
      histogram, loop)

The fp64 node-advance path dominates. The LuaJIT warmup jump-ahead (deferred
item 7) would therefore cap at ~1.3x even if it made the RNG free; it drops
in priority behind removing fp64 divides.

Compiler report (ptxas via --verbose_build, sm_120a):
    95 registers/thread, 0 spill stores/loads, 4464-byte stack frame, 0 barriers

Occupancy is register-bound: 65,536 regs/SM at 96 (allocation granularity)
per thread -> 682 threads -> 21 warps/SM = 672 threads. The measured plateau
at 16 groups of 32 (512 threads) is the largest power-of-two count that fits.
This is also why 64-wide groups were rejected and why the struct shrink moved
nothing: the struct lives in the local-memory stack frame regardless of size,
and registers, not memory, cap resident warps. Cutting below 64 regs/thread
would need the kernel's math restructured; not pursued.

Default -g was briefly raised to 32 groups per CU on the theory that the 21
resident warps per SM want queued work behind them. Measurement disagreed:
erratic_flush_five went 3.02 s -> 3.3 s at 32/SM even with the cheaper
roundDigits, matching the earlier sweep (16/SM 3.55 s, 32/SM 4.0 s). The
default is back at 16 per CU. Observed behaviour wins over the register
arithmetic here.

### Change applied: fp64 divide removed from roundDigits (lib/util.cl)
round(f*1e13)/1e13 became a Markstein-corrected reciprocal:
    q = n*r; e = fma(-q, 1e13, n); q' = fma(e, r, q)   with r = fl(1/1e13)
OpenCL mandates correctly rounded fp64 `/` and fma(), so q' is bit-identical
to n/1e13. (A bare n*r differs in ~20% of cases, which is why it was not
done earlier.) Host verification against `/`: 1.28e9 integer n across
[0, 1e13], 2e7 random n, boundary values, and 1.56e8 chained advances
through the exact get_node_child recurrence — zero mismatches. Replaces one
software fp64 divide (dozens of instructions on consumer NVIDIA) per node
advance with one multiply and two FMAs. Windows golden run still required.

Next candidate: the same treatment for the per-character divide in
pseudohash (1.1239285023/num), which is serially dependent and runs 8 times
per seed in i_new plus once per character of each new node's name.

Measured after the roundDigits change (RTX 5080, same ranges, output
identical to before for all three filters):
    bench_hash_fp64      1.85 s -> 1.58 s   (-15%)
    erratic_flush_five   3.02 s -> 3.3 s    (at 32 groups/CU; see above)
    perkeo_observatory   0.5 s  -> 0.48 s
The hash path gained less than a single-divide-per-advance model predicts,
which suggests the divide was not the whole of that path's cost: fract,
round, and the dependent chain through 51 advances each contribute. The
s_skip fix was also confirmed on a run with no -s: output clean.

